1
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:14,900
Today we're going to continue talking about love, but before, if there is a Nadia in this

2
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:23,560
room and you left a keychain in class, we have it here, so please come and see me afterwards.

3
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:26,260
So let's talk about love.

4
00:00:33,830 --> 00:00:40,480
Last time, we left off with more questions than answers.

5
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:47,480
The question was, difficult question, was how can we sustain love?

6
00:00:48,160 --> 00:01:00,080
How can we sustain passion beyond the honeymoon phase? Because even if we did have our Brad Pitt or Halle Berry, that does not guarantee lasting passion.

7
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:10,220
In fact, what we know that it does guarantee over time is a waning of the physiological excitement that we experience when we are with that person.

8
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:20,160
And that in part explains some of the statistics, such as two-thirds of marriages end up in divorce, such as even those who do stay together.

9
00:01:22,220 --> 00:01:25,500
After a while, it's out of convenience, out of a sense of duty.

10
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:36,580
And not always, or more often than not, does not have the same sense of togetherness, of passion, that existed at the beginning of the relationship,

11
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:45,100
when there were all the good intentions and the hope of a lifelong, committed relationship, marriage or otherwise.

12
00:01:48,800 --> 00:02:00,480
And then we asked what positive psychology can do for us. We asked the additional question to the normal question that has been asked for many years,

13
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:08,120
which is, why do so many relationships fail? It's an important question. It's necessary. It's very far from being sufficient.

14
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:22,920
And it leads to the same errors, to the same shortcomings that asking only the negative question leads to when we're studying at-risk youth, when we're studying time management and stress.

15
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:32,020
Because when we only ask one of the questions, what we're missing are the children on the bus, metaphorically speaking.

16
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:42,820
What we're missing are the colors in the reality in front of us. if the only question that we focus on is how many geometric shapes are there.

17
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:51,160
Questions create reality. And very often, if we don't ask a certain question, certain answers are obscured and disappear.

18
00:02:51,460 --> 00:03:01,660
So we ask this question, and we ask not just about what makes relationships work, but what makes relationships thrive.

19
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:16,600
And psychologists such as David Schnark and John Gottman in their extraordinary books, extraordinary research, talk about some of these characteristics.

20
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:18,240
So let's get into them.

21
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:22,380
The first one is about working hard.

22
00:03:25,860 --> 00:03:28,800
This is the world's best kept secret.

23
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:34,500
It's the secret of success. It's the secret of personal success, of successful relationships.

24
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:45,520
There are no shortcuts. If we want a relationship to thrive, to succeed, we need to put a lot of work in it. Now, that seems self-evident, obvious, common sense.

25
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:51,940
But so often in this class, we have seen what Voltaire has remarked, that common sense is not that common.

26
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:57,220
Now, why is common sense not that common? When it applies to working hard in a relationship.

27
00:03:57,220 --> 00:04:07,500
The reason is because many people have wrong expectations of their relationship or of what it means to have a happy relationship.

28
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:21,100
Specifically, most people believe that the most important part of enjoying a happy, lifelong, committed relationship is finding the right partner.

29
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:30,040
And that's certainly important, but it is a mistake to focus on finding.

30
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:43,860
People have, if we draw an analogy to Carol Dweck's research on fixed versus malleable mindsets, people have a finding mindset that hurts their relationship.

31
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:44,660
Let me explain.

32
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:55,520
So going back, this we did before spring break, what Carol Dweck showed was that when we praise people for their intelligence,

33
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:58,900
Oh, you're so smart after doing well on a test.

34
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:07,160
They enter a fixed mindset versus if we praise them for, oh, you've worked so hard.

35
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:18,260
They have a malleable mindset and that has consequences because the fixed mindset, the intelligence, you're so smart, doesn't help them much.

36
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:29,120
And then when they go to the next puzzle, next task, which is difficult, that threatens their schema. Oh, if I don't get it, if I don't succeed, it means that I'm no longer smart.

37
00:05:30,340 --> 00:05:31,860
And they give up more easily.

38
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:44,120
They're more threatened by failure and they don't enjoy the process. They become perfectionists or more likely to become perfectionists. Okay, so this is the fixed mindset. You're so smart.

39
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:55,160
You're so intelligent. The malleable mindset, oh, you worked so hard. Oh, I can work with that. So even if I don't succeed and don't do well, I can still learn. and I do learn to enjoy the process more.

40
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:57,680
So that's the malleable mindset.

41
00:05:58,510 --> 00:06:01,000
Now let's draw an analogy to what I've just discussed here.

42
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:14,320
If I believe that the most important thing about enjoying a lifelong, committed, happy relationship is finding the right partner,

43
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:17,840
I enter what I call, again, by analogy,

44
00:06:18,670 --> 00:06:22,760
the finding mindset,

45
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:32,620
which would be equivalent to Dweck's fixed mindset.

46
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:39,660
And then let's say I have this mindset, this is the most important thing and then we go through a rough patch in our relationship.

47
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:43,860
Then what happens?

48
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:52,680
Then I begin to think, wait a minute, something's wrong here. I must have not found the right partner.

49
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:57,220
I must have made a mistake, an error of judgment.

50
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:03,000
and that very often leads to a worsening of the situation,

51
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:07,500
and it can, it doesn't always, but can lead to a downward spiral.

52
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:17,300
This is the finding mindset, because it's fixed. I found my knight in shining armor. I found my dream woman.

53
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:19,980
I found my bread pit, my Halle Berry.

54
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:30,120
And you know the problem is that reality inevitably hits. We can't run away from it, and reality dictates that there are no perfect relationships.

55
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:38,640
Remember the Leo Buscalia quote about perfect love is rare indeed. No, it's not rare. It doesn't exist.

56
00:07:39,860 --> 00:07:45,360
And when we realize that, if we have the finding mindset, the fixed mindset,

57
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:47,520
it threatens our schema.

58
00:07:48,380 --> 00:07:53,700
There is inconsistency and then we begin to think, well, I probably did not find the right person.

59
00:07:53,700 --> 00:08:08,360
In contrast, if we have the cultivating mindset, that's malleable.

60
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:18,680
That's related to the effort part. Okay, so we're going through a hardship, we're going through difficulty,

61
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:24,040
but it's okay because we're working on it. We're working to resolve it.

62
00:08:24,700 --> 00:08:30,600
It's like the students who were praised for their effort. Okay, so I'm not solving this puzzle now, but so what?

63
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:35,560
I'm putting in a lot of effort. I'm trying. I'm even enjoying the process.

64
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:44,640
Look at the direct parallel here between Dweck's work on mindset and our mindset when it comes to relationships.

65
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:48,420
So important makes such a big difference.

66
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:55,340
And very often we can switch it, if not immediately, relatively promptly.

67
00:08:56,080 --> 00:09:02,760
Remember, Carol Dweck was able to change the mindset of people in a single experiment, in a few hours.

68
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:12,880
And we can do the same in a relationship if we truly accept and internalize the fact that relationships,

69
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:17,680
while finding the right partner, a good partner, is of course important.

70
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:22,600
It is much more important to then cultivate that relationship.

71
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:31,120
And we'll get to that in a minute. But the question then arises, so why do people have the finding mindset?

72
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:42,520
Why do people have or not have the cultivating mindset if it is healthier? One of the main reasons are the movies.

73
00:09:43,320 --> 00:09:46,500
Now, movies are wonderful. They're terrific. They show us what is possible.

74
00:09:47,100 --> 00:09:55,520
Now, Aristotle once said about art or about fiction that it's more important than history because history depicts things as they are,

75
00:09:56,740 --> 00:10:00,420
whereas fiction depicts things as they can and ought to be.

76
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:12,220
So it's a wonderful thing to show us a model of what's possible, especially the romantic movies. And I don't mean romantic in terms of love. Romantic in the 18th, 19th century sense of romantic,

77
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:19,480
showing us how things can work, how things can become the potential of human nature, of relationships, of our world.

78
00:10:20,100 --> 00:10:29,840
So it's a wonderful thing, But one thing that movies don't do well is that they focus on the finding of the right partner

79
00:10:29,840 --> 00:10:38,460
and hence very often entrench, reinforce the finding mindset. Because most of the movies, love stories look like this.

80
00:10:38,980 --> 00:10:43,520
There are struggles, there are fights, there are disagreements, there are challenges on the way.

81
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:51,060
And then at the end, Mr. and Mrs. Smith get together and they live happily ever after.

82
00:10:54,520 --> 00:10:58,220
The screen drops, and they walk into the sunset.

83
00:11:01,480 --> 00:11:03,560
You know what the problem is with that picture?

84
00:11:06,210 --> 00:11:11,170
The problem is that movies end where love begins.

85
00:11:12,470 --> 00:11:18,830
It is after the screen drops that the actual work starts.

86
00:11:19,290 --> 00:11:29,230
It is easy. It is easy to have a good relationship at the beginning, the honeymoon phase, or through the first year or two, usually.

87
00:11:30,790 --> 00:11:41,880
But then what happens after a while when the initial physical, physiological instinct wanes?

88
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:49,760
What happens when we start to recognize and understand and realize and see that, well, this partner is not perfect?

89
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:59,180
What happens then? Well, that's when true love begins and that's where true love is cultivated and formed.

90
00:12:00,180 --> 00:12:09,400
Now, I'm not belittling the importance of finding a partner, the right partner, the right person for us. It is, of course, important.

91
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:15,820
And often it takes kissing many frogs before we find our prince.

92
00:12:17,970 --> 00:12:22,950
But the more important part of a thriving long-term relationship is the cultivation.

93
00:12:31,060 --> 00:12:33,840
This is where the effort starts.

94
00:12:36,070 --> 00:12:40,030
It's after the sun sets that difficulties and hardships rise.

95
00:12:40,490 --> 00:12:48,150
Now connected to this mindset is the notion that there is only one right person for us.

96
00:12:48,890 --> 00:12:56,210
And all I need to do and all I need to dedicate all my effort and time to is find that right person.

97
00:12:57,910 --> 00:13:03,270
And if I have to go all the way to Yale, I'll go there to find that person.

98
00:13:08,010 --> 00:13:13,170
The modern version of a Shakespearean tragedy, perhaps.

99
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:28,800
Whatever I need to do, I will do it just to find that one right person. Even if I'm in Boston and they're in Seattle, I will find that right person.

100
00:13:31,300 --> 00:13:33,640
I don't believe that. It's simply not true.

101
00:13:35,140 --> 00:13:46,080
And it's not a matter of faith. I mean, we know of many people who thought they found the right person, maybe did find the right person. and then either for tragic reasons or for good reasons

102
00:13:46,080 --> 00:13:51,360
they were no longer together and they found more love. There are six billion people in the world.

103
00:13:51,860 --> 00:13:54,340
There's more than one right person.

104
00:13:56,700 --> 00:13:58,620
So what makes a relationship unique?

105
00:14:00,540 --> 00:14:02,940
It's not finding that one right person.

106
00:14:03,320 --> 00:14:14,680
Once again, it's cultivating that one chosen relationship. It's by virtue of working together, of being together, of spending time together, of dedicating one another.

107
00:14:18,740 --> 00:14:27,460
That's how we create the one chosen relationship, one special relationship. That's how two separate I's become a we.

108
00:14:31,220 --> 00:14:40,520
And again, to do that, it doesn't happen overnight. It doesn't even happen in a year or two. It takes time. In fact, it doesn't happen. It is happening.

109
00:14:41,160 --> 00:14:42,280
It's a process.

110
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:47,640
It's not about success in a relationship. It's about succeeding in a relationship.

111
00:14:53,030 --> 00:15:01,590
We cultivate a relationship not just simply by putting on the ring or by giving the vows.

112
00:15:02,110 --> 00:15:04,370
It's by doing things together.

113
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:15,260
Why is that so important? Let me draw on some research from other areas of psychology. And this is something that I've mentioned on a couple of occasions.

114
00:15:15,260 --> 00:15:39,020
Some of the most important work done by Muzaffar Sharif back in the 1950s, and until Muzaffar Sharif came along, as I mentioned before, the hypothesis that people had when it comes to resolving interpersonal conflicts or intergroup conflicts,

115
00:15:39,020 --> 00:15:43,320
the belief that people had was that all you need to do was get the conflicting sides

116
00:15:43,940 --> 00:15:44,540
together

117
00:15:46,020 --> 00:15:50,560
the dominant hypothesis was what's called the contact hypothesis

118
00:15:52,510 --> 00:16:00,590
get the two conflicting sides in touch with one another whether it's conflicting ethnic groups whether it's conflicting individuals

119
00:16:01,250 --> 00:16:04,130
get them together and you will resolve the conflict

120
00:16:04,750 --> 00:16:07,250
this was much of the work done by Gordon Alport

121
00:16:08,370 --> 00:16:11,570
in our department here back in the 1930s.

122
00:16:12,650 --> 00:16:15,910
Muzaffar Sharif comes along and shows that that's not the case.

123
00:16:16,370 --> 00:16:21,670
In fact, just bringing them together, just the contact of the conflicting sides,

124
00:16:22,190 --> 00:16:27,830
very often, more often than not, intensifies the conflict rather than resolves it.

125
00:16:28,850 --> 00:16:31,890
And I gave this in the context of the Arab-Israeli conflict,

126
00:16:33,530 --> 00:16:35,510
where people just got together

127
00:16:36,030 --> 00:16:41,810
and the hope was that we'll have peace in our time. And instead, we've got more war in our time.

128
00:16:42,590 --> 00:16:43,990
Contact is not enough.

129
00:16:45,650 --> 00:16:50,350
Not when it comes to bringing nations together, not when it comes to bringing ethnic groups together,

130
00:16:51,530 --> 00:16:55,610
and not when it comes to bringing a couple together.

131
00:16:56,850 --> 00:17:01,490
You see, in a relationship, inevitably, there are conflicts.

132
00:17:01,850 --> 00:17:10,190
Inevitably. There is no perfect relationship. And if there were a perfect relationship, it would not be a conflict-free relationship, as we'll see shortly.

133
00:17:10,930 --> 00:17:20,810
So there are conflicts in a relationship, whether it's during the first year or whether it's after two or three years. And serious conflicts. I'm not just saying about, you know, a conflict about,

134
00:17:20,970 --> 00:17:23,570
well, you should put down the toilet seat or keep it up.

135
00:17:24,010 --> 00:17:26,470
I mean serious, well, that could be serious, I guess.

136
00:17:26,890 --> 00:17:28,830
But other serious conflicts.

137
00:17:31,210 --> 00:17:41,170
And when there is conflict and all the couples are doing together is simply being together, in touch with one another, that's not enough to resolve that conflict.

138
00:17:41,350 --> 00:17:49,150
And very often it will start as a minor conflict and then get worse, spiral up or rather spiral down over time.

139
00:17:51,080 --> 00:17:59,220
So what does Sharif say and what do we know today? That the important thing in resolving conflicts is having a superordinate goal.

140
00:18:00,100 --> 00:18:05,880
When the two ethnic groups, when the two nations work together and are interdependent.

141
00:18:06,700 --> 00:18:18,540
Whether it's in business or in a relationship, it could be raising children, but doing the things together or belonging to the same political campaign

142
00:18:18,540 --> 00:18:21,080
or opposite political campaigns, that's fine,

143
00:18:21,720 --> 00:18:25,100
but working together, supporting one another.

144
00:18:25,640 --> 00:18:36,220
And when you have the working together, the doing together, that's when you're most likely to resolve the conflicts. And in fact, these conflicts, as we'll see shortly, can make the relationship even stronger.

145
00:18:37,600 --> 00:18:46,380
What the couple need are mutually meaningful goals. And again, it could be having children together and raising the children together.

146
00:18:46,780 --> 00:18:52,940
You know, not just, okay, well, we have the child and then, you know, the mother or the father raised the kid,

147
00:18:53,300 --> 00:19:00,760
more often the mother, and, okay, so we did something together.

148
00:19:00,760 --> 00:19:09,320
No, the kid has to be, to put it in very delicate and I'm sure appropriate terms, the kid has to be a joint venture.

149
00:19:10,840 --> 00:19:13,640
Isn't that a lovely term to describe having kids?

150
00:19:14,120 --> 00:19:20,540
As long as they don't bring any bad debts or, you know, that there is good return on investment. Yeah, that's just the right language there.

151
00:19:20,980 --> 00:19:22,680
Took me a while to think about it.

152
00:19:23,820 --> 00:19:36,740
So, working together, bringing the kids up together, or being on campaigns together, or helping one another at work, but doing things together.

153
00:19:37,460 --> 00:19:43,000
John Gottman, in the strongest marriages, husband and wife share a deep sense of meaning.

154
00:19:43,240 --> 00:19:51,620
They don't just get along, they also support each other's hopes and aspirations and build a sense of purpose into their lives together.

155
00:19:52,920 --> 00:20:01,960
That doesn't mean that they need to do everything together. And it's okay for one to have his or her own meaningful goals at work where they don't work together. That's perfectly fine.

156
00:20:02,280 --> 00:20:05,780
But in addition, there have to be things that are done together.

157
00:20:08,120 --> 00:20:19,540
In other words, what they need is an active kind of love. The first time I really understood the meaning of active love was through a story that Shahar, who is one of my best friends in Israel, told me.

158
00:20:20,320 --> 00:20:31,380
We were in the same class, but he got married at a relatively young age. He was in his early 20s and had his first kid soon after.

159
00:20:32,100 --> 00:20:35,800
And he's very introspective, very honest, high levels of integrity.

160
00:20:36,560 --> 00:20:42,000
And he told me that at the beginning, when he had Noam,

161
00:20:43,820 --> 00:20:48,660
when Noam came home from hospital with his wife, with his mom,

162
00:20:50,200 --> 00:20:56,400
And Shahar didn't really feel love toward Noam.

163
00:20:56,840 --> 00:21:06,240
He was very cute, adorable, but he didn't feel a strong sense of love that he thought he should have felt. Needless to say, he felt enormously guilty about it.

164
00:21:08,620 --> 00:21:10,900
That didn't last for long, fortunately.

165
00:21:11,420 --> 00:21:22,020
Because as soon as Shahar started taking care of Noam, which meant changing his diapers which meant getting up in the middle of the night

166
00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:29,020
and feeding him which meant taking him for walks which meant cleaning after him

167
00:21:31,070 --> 00:21:37,310
that's when the love was cultivated that's when he started to feel a deep sense

168
00:21:38,170 --> 00:21:46,650
of fatherhood for the first time so it wasn't natural for some people it is natural for women more often but not always more often than men

169
00:21:49,570 --> 00:21:52,750
So we can rely and wait for this natural thing to happen,

170
00:21:54,810 --> 00:22:01,250
or we can actively engage in activities that show, that illustrate our love, and remember self-perception theory.

171
00:22:03,240 --> 00:22:08,380
We very often form theories and beliefs about ourselves based on how we behave.

172
00:22:09,300 --> 00:22:14,380
So the examples we used in the past, if I see myself going up to someone and asking them out,

173
00:22:14,920 --> 00:22:21,860
even if they say no, I perceive myself as someone with confidence, and my self-esteem will go up, regardless of the response.

174
00:22:23,320 --> 00:22:30,370
If I see myself trying out for plays, I will perceive myself as someone courageous.

175
00:22:32,010 --> 00:22:36,250
If I perceive myself as, if I take care of myself,

176
00:22:38,200 --> 00:22:45,220
I'll see myself as someone with self-esteem who cares about him or herself. So, the same with children.

177
00:22:45,220 --> 00:22:54,260
If I see myself taking care of my child, my conclusion in self-perception theory is, oh, I must love that child.

178
00:22:54,460 --> 00:22:57,280
And that love actually intensifies over time.

179
00:22:57,660 --> 00:23:00,720
Whether it's with children, whether it's with partners.

180
00:23:02,700 --> 00:23:07,080
We may experience the love automatically at the beginning. It could be love at first sight.

181
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:16,200
However, if we don't sustain the act of love, over time, because of self-perception theory, over time that will go away.

182
00:23:17,760 --> 00:23:26,160
Because if I don't invest anything in the relationship, self-perception theory, oh, it must not be that important for you.

183
00:23:27,240 --> 00:23:32,340
And then the love wanes, not to mention all the physiological reasons why it wanes over time.

184
00:23:33,960 --> 00:23:37,540
So if there isn't the act of love, it cannot be sustained.

185
00:23:38,380 --> 00:23:43,440
How do we sustain love? How do we remain active? Relationships, rituals.

186
00:23:43,440 --> 00:23:58,860
Now, when we talked about rituals, I told you about the rituals that my wife and I had, have two dates a week, meals with the whole family, additional at least once a week, usually it's three or four times a week.

187
00:23:59,020 --> 00:24:13,000
But we have these rituals, because without rituals in our modern world, the important yet not urgent, in the words of Stephen Covey, the important yet not urgent activities will take a back seat.

188
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:23,060
So, yeah, we know it's important to go on dates, but it's not as urgent as a phone call that I just received from work and I have to wait or stay at work.

189
00:24:23,660 --> 00:24:28,420
Whereas if it's a ritual, if we do it no matter what, then it will sustain itself.

190
00:24:28,420 --> 00:24:45,010
The second way and extremely important way of sustaining love

191
00:24:45,010 --> 00:24:53,930
is making that shift from the desire to be validated to the desire to be known.

